  /reload-plugins
  /codex:setup

  Proceeding with my own approaches.

  ---

  [1mApproaches: where change events come from[0m

  This is the fork that decides whether notifications are cheap or a rewrite later.

  [1mA. Event log written inside the task transaction[0m [3m(recommended)[0m
  Every task mutation writes a row to an [38;5;153mevents[39m table in the same SQLite transaction as the task row itself. The events
  table is append-only: [38;5;153mid, task_id, actor_id, kind, payload_json, created_at[39m. Notifications in sub-project #4 become a
  read over this table plus a per-user cursor.
  [3mTrade-off:[0m a small amount of discipline in sub-project #1 — every write path goes through one function. Buys exact
  consistency (no task change can exist without its event), a natural audit log, and makes #3 almost free.

  [1mB. Derive changes by diffing snapshots[0m
  Store task versions or compare old/new on write, computing "what changed" at read time.
  [3mTrade-off:[0m no upfront discipline, but reconstructing [3mwho[0m changed [3mwhat[0m and [3mwhen[0m gets expensive and lossy. Deletes are
  especially awkward. I don't recommend it.

  [1mC. Defer entirely — add events in sub-project #3[0m
  Build plain task CRUD now, retrofit the event log later.
  [3mTrade-off:[0m fastest to a working task list. But retrofitting means revisiting every write path anyway, and any task
  history from before the retrofit is permanently missing. The saved effort is borrowed, not avoided.

  I recommend [1mA[0m. The discipline it asks for in #1 is one module and a rule; C pays the same cost later plus a data gap.

  Does that hold, or do you want to push back on any of it? If it holds, I'll present the full design for sub-project #1
  in sections — data model, API surface, auth/session handling, the write-path module, and testing — and get your
  sign-off on each before writing anything to [38;5;153mdocs/hyperpowers/specs/[39m.

